  { q: "What's the most common LXe undercounter dishwasher failure?", a: "Door gasket compression set is the #1 Hobart LXe / LXeR / LXn / LXnR service item across LA commercial undercounter installations. Gasket wears with continuous thermal cycling from 180°F final rinse exposure, loses compression seal, water sprays out during wash cycle. Year 5-7 typical wear curve. $180-$340 gasket replacement, 45-60 minutes. Plus door switch wear (year 5-7, $140-$240) often paired with gasket replacement. Other top LXe / LXeR service items: drain pump (year 7-9, $280-$440), fill solenoid (year 7-9, $200-$340), heating element (year 6-8, $240-$380), wash arm bearing (year 5-8, $180-$320). LXe rackless variant (LXeR / LXnR) common in West Hollywood + Arts District bar cluster for glass and small-ware operations — same failure pattern profile as standard LXe. ITW Food Equipment Group Ohio parts pipeline 5-10 day standard ship; common LXe parts stocked on truck for first-visit completion." },
  {
    q: "My undercounter dishwasher cycle stops short. What's wrong?",
    a: "Most common cause is a worn door gasket failing the door switch interlock (year 4 to 7 typical). Less common: water inlet valve degraded, drain pump clogged with debris, control board fault. We diagnose with the front access panel removed, $120 commercial diagnostic."
  },
  {
    q: "Do you service Hobart LXeR, CMA Energy Mizer, and Champion UH series?",
    a: "Yes. These three platforms cover roughly 75% of our undercounter calls. Plus Jackson Conserver. We carry common door gaskets, wash pump impellers, drain pumps, and detergent dispenser solenoids on the van for these models."
  },
  {
    q: "What's the difference between residential and commercial undercounter?",
    a: "Residential undercounter dishwashers run on 120V, cycle in 60+ minutes, lower wash temp, NSF residential rating. Commercial undercounter (Hobart LXeR, CMA, Champion UH) runs on 208V or 240V three-phase, 90-second to 4-minute cycles, higher wash temps, NSF 184 commercial rating, integrated chemical sanitizing or high-temp final rinse. Different repair scope."
  },
